\section{Einsum Examples}
\label{app:einsum}
Einstein summation~\footnote{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Einstein\_notation} is the notation for the summation of the product of elements in a list of high-dimensional tensors.
We found the aggregation of grounding messages w.r.t. an implication can be exactly represented by an Einstein summation expression.
Notably, Einstein summation can be efficiently implemented in parallel via NumPy and nowadays deep learning frameworks, e.g., PyTorch and TensorFlow.
The corresponding function is called $\mathtt{einsum}$~\footnote{https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.einsum.html} which can be effectively optimized via a library \texttt{opt\_einsum}~\footnote{https://optimized-einsum.readthedocs.io/en/stable/}. 
We list several cases of message aggregation in the Einstein summation format and their optimal simplifications via dynamic argument contraction.
Deriving the optimal solution is an NP-hard problem, but for the practical formulas whose argument size (i.e., arity) is less than 6, the solution can be obtained within milliseconds.
For all the formulas in the tested datasets, the number of arguments is less than 6.


