\subsubsection{State-sequences}
As explained in Section~\ref{sec_acq_normal_state_seq}, we define a new state by combining selected signals in a three-level hierarchy. First, we focus on the primary outputs: nine control signals in the convolutional layer module connected to the “Control module” and “Direct Memory Access module.” These signals include module state, communication (ready, valid), and data address bits. Combined, they form 79 bits.

From an RTL simulation of a $32\times32\times1$ activation input, we obtain 322 normal state sequences using Algorithm~\ref{alg_normal_seq}. An error detector using all 79 bits consumes 29.3\% of the circuit area, which is often impractical. Hence, we reduce overhead by selecting one bit from each of the nine outputs, creating a 9-bit state. Similarly, we choose one bit from other monitored signals. We typically pick either the most or least significant bit or restrict our choice to the bit range in use, then take the top or bottom bit in that range. Similar analyses are conducted for the remaining hierarchies.

