\subsection{Network-on-Chip (NoC) router}
The final target is a Network-on-Chip (NoC) router~\cite{Kyle}, an open-source on-chip router originally developed in~\cite{Matsutani2009}. Its architecture is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_router}. The router connects in five directions—north, east, south, west, and injection—in a mesh-structured NoC. The directional ports connect to neighboring routers, while the injection port links to a directly interfacing module.
%
Each port has identical input/output signals: "data" for transferred data, "ack" for acknowledgment, "lck" for lock status, "valid" for valid data, and "vch" for the virtual channel, with two virtual channels per port. All ports include an input unit with a FIFO and a routing computation module. YX routing with static arbitration is used, prioritizing multicast over unicast. The routing priority is injection, west, south, east, and north.
The crossbar module, a multiplexer-based controller, handles data transmission. Each port also includes an output unit managing FIFO data ejection and virtual channel switching. Output signals mirror input meanings, with "rdy" indicating the router's ready status.
\if 0
The last target is a Network on Chip (NoC) router \cite{Kyle}, an open-source on-chip router originally developed for \cite{Matsutani2009}.
Fig.~\ref{fig_router} illustrates the router architecture.
The router has connections to five directions: north, east, south, west, and injection, in a mesh-structured NoC. The north, east, south, and west ports are connected to neighboring routers, while the injection port is connected to a module that directly interfaces with the router.
The inputs and outputs are the same for each direction. The inputs include ``data" for transferred data, ``ack" for acknowledgment of data reception, ``lck" indicating the locking status, ``valid" signaling when the input data is valid, and ``vch" representing the virtual channel in use, with two virtual channels implemented per physical port.
Each directional port has an input unit with the same configuration, which includes a FIFO for data storage and a routing computation module. YX routing is implemented with static arbitration, where multicast transmissions are given higher priority than unicast transmissions. The priority order for directional routing is as follows: injection, west, south, east, and north.
The crossbar module is a multiplexer-based controller for data transmission. The output units are implemented for each direction with the same configuration. These units manage data ejection from FIFOs and the switching of virtual channels. The output signals, including ``data," ``ack," ``lck," ``valid," and ``vch," have the same meanings as their corresponding input signals. Additionally, the ``rdy" signal indicates the ready status of the router itself. 
