The NoC consists of a 4~$\times$~4 mesh-structured network of routers. The four routers in the leftmost column are assumed to be connected to the universal control units of the NoC, which are described in\cite{Shao2019} as a RISC-V processor and a global processing element. The remaining routers are assumed to be connected to processing elements responsible for multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations.

The simulation scenario models data transmission in the CNN accelerator, as implemented in~\cite{Kyle}.
Multicast transmission is performed from Router~2 to six routers (Router~6, 10, 14, 7, 11, and 15), representing the activation data transfer from a control unit to MAC processing elements.
Furthermore, following the multicast transmission from Router~2, and after a predefined latency, unicast transmissions are executed from Router~6 to Router~7, Router~10 to Router~11, and Router~14 to Router~15. This flow simulates the data transfer of computation results between MAC processing elements.
Thus, the latency between the initiation of unicast transmissions reflects the computation latency of MAC operations.  
Specifically, Router~2 transmits 128 packets, each consisting of 9 flits, using multicast transmission. Each flit is 66 bits, comprising 2 bits for the flit type and 64 bits for data.
During unicast transmissions, a total of 256 flits are transferred. 
The latency between the initiation of unicast transmissions is predefined as 1,024 cycles, assuming 128 MAC operations, originally referring to ~\cite{Shao2019}.
To achieve high-throughput data transmission, two virtual channels are utilized. The virtual channels are alternately assigned to each flit to mitigate congestion and enhance transmission efficiency.

In the test scenario, Router~2 is monitored using Petri nets and normal state transitions.
