\section{Conclusion}\label{sec_conclusion}
This paper presented two control-flow-based error detection methods: one using Petri nets generated from specifications and the other using state sequences derived from runtime execution. We developed a methodology for implementing both Petri net detectors and state sequence checkers, validated through fault injection on a convolutional layer, Gaussian blur, AES encryption, and a NoC router. Detection rates ranged from 48\% to 100\% for both register bit-flips and primary input faults, whereas simple register duplication cannot detect errors caused by primary input faults.
%
Maximum detection was achieved with area overheads of only a few percent to around 10\% in most cases. By selectively applying these methods, designers can explore the reliability-area trade-off.

\if 0
This paper presented two error detection methods based on monitoring control flow using Petri nets generated from specifications and using state sequences obtained from runtime execution. We developed a methodology for implementing Petri-net-based detectors and state sequence checkers. Our methodology was validated through fault injection tests on the convolutional layer, Gaussian blur, AES encryption, and NoC router, achieving a detection rate of 48\% to 100\% for both register bit-flips and primary input failures. The maximum error detection rate is achieved with an area overhead of a few to around 10 \% in most cases. This approach detects errors caused by both bit-flip and input failures whereas simple register duplication cannot detect input failures. By selectively applying our proposed methods, a wide range of area constraints can be considered to enhance reliability.
\fi

