Table~\ref{tab:motion-ablation} investigates the effectiveness of different motion models combined with our association metrics. The UKF consistently outperforms other motion models including standard Kalman Filter (KF), Adaptive Kalman Filter (AKF), and Strong Tracking Filter (STF) across both HMIoU~\cite{yang2024hybrid} and FishIoU association. This validates our hypothesis that non-linear motion models are more appropriate for capturing the complex swimming patterns of fish. The best performance is achieved by combining UKF with FishIoU and Re-ID, resulting in a HOTA of 34.1 and IDF1 of 44.6.

Table~\ref{tab:sequence-performance} presents the performance of our SU-T baseline across individual sequences in the MFT25 dataset, revealing substantial variation in tracking difficulty. The PF-002 sequence demonstrates exceptional performance with 65.2 HOTA, 98.7 IDF1, and none IDs, highlighting the effectiveness of SU-T under favorable conditions with distinct fish appearances and predictable movements. Similarly, BT-004 achieves strong results with 51.2 HOTA and 68.9 IDF1, demonstrating robust tracking capabilities in moderately challenging scenarios where fish maintain relatively consistent swimming patterns.

In contrast, sequences like SN-011 exhibit significantly lower performance with 20.3 HOTA, due to challenging conditions including numerous occlusions and highly erratic movements. MSK-003 shows moderate 34.2 HOTA but suffers from higher 134 IDs, indicating difficulties in maintaining consistent identities in crowded environments. SN-009 and SN-011 both demonstrate substantial identification failures, with high IDFN values of 30,853 and 23,575, respectively. This reveals the limitations of current tracking approaches in visibility-constrained underwater environments.

To visualize the practical effectiveness of SU-T, Fig.~\ref{fig:vis-ours} presents tracking results across diverse scenarios in the MFT25 dataset. The visualizations demonstrate that SU-T maintains consistent identity assignments despite varying challenges including different fish densities, species morphologies, and environmental conditions.

