\section{Methodology}

Our methodology advances low-resource opinion-expression assessment through three key innovations. First, we introduce a proficiency-aware text synthesis module that leverages an LLM to generate response texts closely aligned with each proficiency level. This module constructs in-context prompts by sampling and permuting transcripts of real test-takers' responses, ensuring that generated responses faithfully mimic authentic test-takers' language competence. Second, we design a speaker-conditioned voice-cloning pipeline, leveraging embeddings extracted from real recordings to guide a multi-speaker TTS model to produce synthesized utterances that preserve non-native prosody and natural hesitations. Third, we design and implement a novel dynamic importance loss, which automatically adjusts the importance weight of each training instance based on the relative confidence of a fixed “quality” model and the trainable “target” model. This concept parallels the data-weighting idea of Kuo et al. in text data generation \cite{kuo2025not}, but our formulation is the first to integrate it into a fully end-to-end multimodal scoring network. We implement this loss within our scoring model, a Phi-4 multimodal \footnotemark[1], enhanced with LoRA adapters \cite{hu2022lora} for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. Together, these three innovations—rich text augmentation, authentic speech cloning, and principled loss reweighting—curate a training dataset that balances the diversity and fidelity factors between synthesized and real data, yielding robust scoring performance under stringent data constraints.

\subsection{LLM-Based Text Synthesis}

To simulate responses from test-takers of different proficiency levels and enrich the diversity of our synthesized training instances, we leverage the in‐context learning capability of an LLM. For each target level $l$, we first randomly select $k$ transcribed responses from the genuine training dataset, denoted as $\{R_{l,1}, R_{l,2}, \dots, R_{l,k}\}$. We then concatenate the task prompt $Q$ with these examples in order to form an input prompt:

\begin{equation}
P_l = \bigl[Q;\,R_{l,1};\,R_{l,2};\dots;\,R_{l,k}\bigr],
\end{equation}
accompanied by the instruction, “Please generate your response in the style of the above examples.” During decoding, we set a relatively high temperature $\tau$ so that

\begin{equation}
\hat{R_{l,i}} \sim \mathrm{LLM}\bigl(P_l;\,\tau\bigr),\quad i=1,\dots,n,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{R_{l,i}}$ is the $i$-th synthesized response and $n$ is the number of instances generated per level. By shuffling the order of the examples and repeating this generation process across multiple rounds, we obtain a synthesized dataset of text responses that remains aligned with the prompt’s content while exhibiting rich variation in vocabulary, syntax, and viewpoints.

