\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose SmartThinker, a two-stage learnable framework that enables fine-grained control over the length of reasoning chains based on step-level importance, which effectively mitigates reward hacking caused by global length penalty and achieves a better trade-off between reasoning accuracy and computational efficiency.

    \item We introduce SCPO, which leverages step-level importance to generate dense process reward signals, guiding the model to penalize the length of redundant steps more strongly while relaxing constraints on critical ones.

    \item We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of SmartThinker in reducing redundant reasoning, and detailed ablation studies further confirm the contribution of each component within the framework.
\end{itemize}


\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Efficient Reasoning Models}
%推理模型通过扩展思维链来增强解决复杂问题的能力，但过长的思维链往往会带来冗余的 token 生成，浪费大量计算资源。为了解决这一问题，已有研究尝试在不牺牲正确率的前提下提升推理模型的效率。这些研究大致可以分为三类：基于 SFT 的方法、基于偏好优化的方法，以及基于强化学习的方法。基于 SFT 的方法，通常通过构造合成的短思维链数据来训练模型，使其更好地适应短推理模式，或者直接训练模型在推理时动态压缩自身的思维过程，从而实现更高效的推理。基于偏好优化的方法，例如 DPO、SimPO 等，则通过构建长短思维链的偏好数据对，引导模型生成更符合人类偏好的短思维链。基于强化学习的方法是目前较为主流的方向，这类方法通常设计精细的奖励函数，并在其中引入对推理链整体长度的惩罚，以促使模型生成更简洁的回答。然而，这种针对思维链整体长度的惩罚策略，往往忽略了不同推理步骤之间的重要性差异，导致模型在压缩推理链时缺乏针对性，甚至可能盲目删减关键步骤，从而影响推理的质量和效果。与之不同，SmartThinker 则利用不同推理步骤的重要性差异，对冗余步骤施加更严格的长度惩罚，而对关键步骤则适当放宽限制，因而在推理效率与正确率之间实现了更优的平衡。

% Reasoning models improve their ability to solve complex problems by generating extended chains of thought \cite{ye2025limo,chen2025towards}. However, excessively long reasoning chains often lead to redundant token generation and substantial waste of computational resources \cite{feng2025efficient,kumar2025overthink,fu2024efficiently}. To address this issue, prior research has explored ways to improve the efficiency of reasoning models without sacrificing accuracy \cite{sui2025stop,qu2025survey}. These approaches can be broadly categorized into three groups: methods based on SFT, preference optimization, and RL. SFT-based methods typically involve constructing synthetic datasets with shorter chains of thought to train models to better adapt to concise reasoning patterns \cite{xia2025tokenskip,han2024token,xu2025chain}. Alternatively, they directly train models to dynamically compress their reasoning processes during inference, thereby achieving more efficient reasoning \cite{zhang2025lightthinker,yan2025inftythink}. Methods based on preference optimization guide models to generate shorter chains of thought that align more closely with human preferences by constructing paired preference data comparing long and short reasoning paths \cite{kumar2025overthink,han2024token}. RL has emerged as a mainstream approach in this area \cite{aggarwal2025l1,arora2025training,hou2025thinkprune,zhang2025grpo}. These methods generally design sophisticated reward functions that incorporate penalty for the overall length of the reasoning chain, encouraging models to produce more concise responses. However, penalizing the overall length of chains often overlooks the varying importance of different reasoning steps, causing models to lack precision when compressing their reasoning. This can result in the indiscriminate removal of critical steps, which ultimately compromises the quality and effectiveness of reasoning. In contrast, SmartThinker explicitly accounts for the varying importance of individual reasoning steps. It imposes stricter length penalty on redundant steps while relaxing constraints on critical ones. As a result, SmartThinker achieves a more optimal balance between reasoning efficiency and accuracy.

% Reasoning models enhance their ability to tackle complex problems by generating extended chains of thought \cite{ye2025limo,chen2025towards}. However, overly long reasoning chains often produce redundant tokens and waste substantial computational resources \cite{kumar2025overthink,fu2024efficiently}. To mitigate this, prior work has focused on improving reasoning efficiency without sacrificing accuracy \cite{sui2025stop,qu2025survey}. Existing methods generally fall into two categories: SFT-based approaches and RL. SFT-based methods either create synthetic datasets with shorter reasoning chains to train models toward concise reasoning \cite{xia2025tokenskip,han2024token,xu2025chain} or directly teach models to compress their reasoning dynamically during inference \cite{zhang2025lightthinker,yan2025inftythink}. Meanwhile, RL has become a mainstream strategy, designing reward functions that penalize longer chains to encourage concise outputs \cite{aggarwal2025l1,arora2025training,hou2025thinkprune,zhang2025grpo}. However, simply penalizing overall chain length ignores the varying importance of individual reasoning steps, often leading models to indiscriminately remove crucial steps and thus compromise reasoning quality. In contrast, SmartThinker explicitly models the varying importance of different steps, imposing stronger penalties on redundant steps while relaxing constraints on critical ones, thereby achieving a better balance between reasoning efficiency and accuracy.

Reasoning models improve their ability to solve complex problems by generating extended chains of thought \cite{ye2025limo,chen2025towards}. However, excessively long reasoning chains often introduce redundancy and waste computational resources \cite{kumar2025overthink,fu2024efficiently}. To address this, prior work has focused on enhancing reasoning efficiency without sacrificing accuracy \cite{sui2025stop,qu2025survey}. Existing methods mainly fall into two categories: SFT-based approaches and RL. SFT-based methods either create synthetic datasets with shorter reasoning chains to train models for concise reasoning \cite{xia2025tokenskip,han2024token,xu2025chain} or teach models to dynamically compress reasoning during inference \cite{zhang2025lightthinker,yan2025inftythink}. Meanwhile, RL has emerged as a popular strategy, designing reward functions that penalize longer chains to encourage brevity \cite{aggarwal2025l1,arora2025training,hou2025thinkprune}. However, simply penalizing overall chain length overlooks the varying importance of individual reasoning steps, often causing models to eliminate crucial steps and compromise reasoning quality. In contrast, SmartThinker explicitly models the importance of different steps, imposing stronger penalties on redundant ones while relaxing constraints on critical steps, thus achieving a better balance between efficiency and accuracy.





