	\subsection{Warm-up 1: parsing trees}


	Let $T$ be a tree, and let $u,v \in V(T)$. Let $P_{uv}$ be the path from~$u$ to~$v$ in~$T$. Let $m$ be the number of vertices of $P_{uv}$, and let $w_1, \ldots, w_m$ be the vertices of $P_{uv}$ (with $w_1 = u$ and $w_m = v$). For each $i \in \{1, \ldots, m\}$, the tree $T_{i}$ \emph{rooted in~$w_i$} is the connected component of~$w_i$ in $T$ after removing all the edges of $P_{uv}$ (with a prescribed vertex $w_i$). Finally, let $\sigma(T,u,v)$ be the sequence of rooted trees $T_1, \ldots, T_m$. We say that $\sigma(T,u,v)$ is a \emph{parsing} of the tree~$T$. See Figure~\ref{fig:parsing} for an example.

