		First, let us prove that $\P$ can be certified with certificates of size $O(f(n))$. The certificates given by the prover to the vertices are the following. The prover gives a special certificate to every vertex of degree~$1$ (note that, on correct instances, a vertex belongs to~$P$ if and only if it has degree at least~$2$). Then, for each $i \in \{1, \ldots, d\}$, the prover writes~$i$ in binary in the certificate of~$u_i$. This uses $O(\log d)$ bits. Since the total number of vertices~$n$ is equal to $\sum_{i=1}^{d} a_i$, the size of the certificates is~$O(f(n))$ because $\log d \leqslant f\left(\sum_{i=1}^{d}a_i\right)$.
		The verification of the vertices consists simply in checking that they have the special certificate if and only if they have degree~$1$, and otherwise they check that their certificate is consistent with the certificates of their neighbors of degree at least~$2$ (for instance, if a vertex has certificate~$i$ and has two neighbors of degree at least~$2$ with certificates~$j$ and~$\ell$, it checks that~$\{j,\ell\} = \{i-1,i+1\}$). Finally, every vertex of degree at least~$2$ with certificate~$i$ checks that it has~$a_i-1$ neighbors with the certificate of degree~$1$ vertices.

		%\textcolor{red}{N. Tentative (preuve précédente en commentaire).} Let us now prove that it is not possible to certify~$\P$ with certificates of size~$o(f(n))$. Assume by contradiction that it is possible to certify~$\P$ with certificates of size~$g(n)$ where $g(n)=o(f(n))$. Let us consider the minimum integer $d$ such that $2^{2g(d)}+1 \le 2^{f(d)}$, such a $d$ exists definition of $g$.  Let $G$ be the caterpillar in~$\P$ whose central path~$P$ has length~$2^{f(d)}$ and let $c$ be an assignment of certificates such that all the vertices accept. Since there are only $2^{2g(d)}$ \textcolor{blue}{il faudrait que le nombre de sommets soit $d$ pour que ça soit vrai ?} pairs of distinct certificates, there exist $i,j$, with $1 \leqslant i < j \leqslant d$, such that $(c(u_i),c(u_{i+1})) = (c(u_j), c(u_{j+1}))$. Now, let us consider the caterpillar~$G'$ obtained from $G$ by deleting the part between~$u_{i+1}$ and~$u_j$. Note that the central path $P'$ of $G'$ has length $d-j+i$. Then, all the vertices of $G'$ accept with the same certificates (more precisely, with the restriction of~$c$ to the vertices of~$G'$), because they have the same view as a vertex which accepts in~$G$. However, $G'$ does not satisfy~$\P$. Indeed the vertices in $P'$ have $a_1, \ldots, a_i, a_{j+1}, \ldots, a_d$ neighbors of degree~$1$, and $a_{i+1} \neq a_{j+1}$ since $i < j$ and $(a_n)$ is increasing.
		%\textcolor{blue}{S: je pense qu'il y a un bug dans la preuve précédente. Avec un chemin central de longueur $d$, on a $n_d$ vertices ($n_d = d + \sum_{i=1}^d a_i$), et seulement $O(\log d)$ bits sont suffisants pour la certification. Maintenant on pourrait très bien avoir $\log d = o(f(n_d))$ si les $a_i$ sont très grands, et dans ce cas on a une certification en $o(\log f(n))$... peut-être qu'on pourrait s'en sortir en choisissant bien les $a_i$ ?}

		%\textcolor{blue}{En fait la preuve montre qu'il faut au moins $\Omega(\log d)$ bits. Pour avoir une borne inf en $\Omega(f(n))$ on aurait donc besoin d'avoir $f(n) = O(\log d)$. Il faudrait donc bien choisir les $a_i$ mais des hypothèses supplémentaires sur $f$ sont peut-être nécessaires pour cela...}
		Let us now prove that it is not possible to certify~$\P$ with certificates of size~$o(f(n))$. Assume by contradiction that certificates of size~$o(f(n))$ are sufficient.
		For every $d \geqslant 1$ such that $a_d \geqslant 2$ (since $(a_i)_{i \in \N}$ is unbounded, there exists infinitely many such integers~$d$), let us denote by~$G_d$ the caterpillar satisfying~$\P$ having its central path of length~$d$, by $u_1, \ldots, u_d$ the vertices of its central path, and by $n_d := \sum_{i=1}^d a_i$ its number of vertices.
		By inequality~(\ref{eq:lemma a_i}), we have $f(n_d)=\Theta(\log d)$. Thus, the size of the certificates that makes all the vertices of~$G_d$ accept is $o(\log d)$. So there exists $d \geqslant 1$ such that $a_d \geqslant 2$ and the number of different pairs of certificates is strictly smaller than $d-1$.

		Now, let us fix such an integer $d$, let $M = \max_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant d} a_i$, and finally let $d' \in \N$ be such that $a_{d'} > M+1$ (such an integer $d'$ exists because $(a_i)_{i \in \N}$ is unbounded). Consider the $(d-1)$ pairs of consecutive certificates $c(u_1), \ldots, c(u_d)$ attributed by the prover to $u_1, \ldots, u_d$ in~$G_d$ in an assignment of certificates such that every vertex accepts. By the pigeonhole principle, there exists $1 \leqslant k < \ell \leqslant d$ such that $(c(u_k),c(u_{k+1})) = (c(u_{\ell}), c(u_{\ell+1}))$. For every $t \in \N$, we can copy $t$ times the part between $u_k$ and $u_\ell$ (with the same certificates) to obtain a new caterpillar in which every vertex accepts because it has the same view as a vertex in~$G_d$ which accepts. See Figure~\ref{fig:pumping_caterpillar} for an example in which $t=2$. In particular, by choosing $t$ large enough, the caterpillar we obtain has its central path of length at least $d'$, and is accepted with these certificates. However, the vertex $u_{d'}$ should have~$a_{d'}-1$ neighbors of degree~1, but it has most $M$ such neighbors (by definition of~$M$) and $M < a_{d'}-1$. This is a contradiction and concludes the proof. 
		\qedhere

