\begin{document}
\begin{abstract}
\citet{erdosCrossingNumberProblems1973}~initiated a line of research studying $\mu_k(n)$, the minimum number of convex $k$-gons one can obtain by placing $n$ points in the plane without any three of them being collinear. Asymptotically, the limits $c_k := \lim_{n\to \infty} \mu_k(n)/\binom{n}{k}$ exist for all $k$, and are strictly positive due to the Erd\H{o}s-Szekeres theorem.
This article focuses on the case $k=5$, where $c_5$ was known to be between $0.0608516$ and $0.0625$~\citep{goaoc2018limitsordertypes, subercaseaux2023minimizing}. The lower bound was obtained through the Flag Algebra method of~\citet{razborovFlagAlgebras2007}, using semi-definite programming. 
In this article we prove a more modest lower bound of $\frac{5\sqrt{5}-11}{4} \approx 0.04508$ without any computation; we exploit \emph{``planar-point equations''}  that count, in different ways, the number of convex pentagons (or other geometric objects) in a point placement.  To derive our lower bound we combine such equations by viewing them from a statistical perspective, which we believe can be fruitful for other related problems.
\end{abstract}


