    In the above discussion, the centers $q_1, q_2, q_3, q_4\in S$ were chosen arbitrarily from the corresponding gaps between $\alpha_i$, $\beta_1'$, and $\beta_2'$. We now choose some particular centers. Let $q_1$ be the center of $S$ such that its group $G_{q_1}$ contains the sublist of $\calA$ immediately following $\beta_1'$ clockwise, $q_2$ the center such that its group $G_{q_2}$ contains the sublist of $\calA$ immediately following $\beta_1'$ counterclockwise, $q_3$ the center such that its group  $G_{q_3}$ contains the sublist of $\calA$ immediately following $\beta_2'$ clockwise, and $q_4$ the center such that its group $G_{q_4}$ contains the sublist of $\calA$ immediately following $\beta_2'$ counterclockwise. 
