\section{Conclusion}
We have presented an adaptive determinantal scheduling framework for wireless networks. By formulating the scheduling problem within the context of proportional fairness and leveraging $L$-ensembles, we have demonstrated that determinantal processes offer a mathematically elegant and computationally tractable approach for network scheduling.
%Our results validate that determinantal scheduling can outperform traditional opportunistic Aloha protocols while maintaining proportional fairness. The repulsive nature of determinantal processes allows for better spatial distribution of active transmitters, reducing interference and improving overall network performance. 
Furthermore, the proposed approach is adaptable to various network configurations without requiring repeated optimization for each new network deployment.

Promising directions for future research emerge from this work.
\textbf{Training on pre-optimized networks}: Using pre-optimized networks to trains models with determinantal fairness framework  is the first obvious step. 
%This could shed light on which parametric determinantal models are suitable, as well as giving insight into which training methods to use.  
%\item \textbf{Dynamic network adaptation:} Extending the current framework to handle time-varying network conditions and user mobility would enhance practical applicability. This could involve developing online learning algorithms that dynamically adjust the quality vectors based on changing network states.
 \textbf{Multi-objective optimization:} Incorporating additional performance metrics beyond proportional fairness, such as energy efficiency, delay constraints, or quality-of-service requirements, could lead to more comprehensive scheduling solutions that balance multiple competing objectives.
\textbf{Distributed scheduling:} Developing decentralized versions of determinantal scheduling algorithms would aid their deployment in large-scale networks where centralized control is impractical. %This might involve approximation techniques that maintain the repulsive properties while allowing for localized decision-making.
\textbf{Leveraging machine learning:} Further exploration of the connection between determinantal processes and machine learning techniques could yield powerful approaches. 
%Deep learning methods could be used to identify optimal feature representations for quality vectors or to predict network performance under different scheduling policies.
\textbf{Theoretical performance bounds:} Establishing theoretical guarantees on the performance gap between optimal scheduling and determinantal approximations would provide valuable insights into the fundamental limits of determinantal scheduling approaches.
%\item \textbf{Application to heterogeneous networks:} Extending the framework to heterogeneous networks with different types of transmitters and access points could address the increasing complexity of modern wireless ecosystems, particularly in 5G networks and beyond.

In conclusion, determinantal point processes offer a promising mathematical foundation for wireless network scheduling that balances computational tractability with sophisticated spatial modelling. %By continuing to explore the theoretical properties and practical implementations of determinantal scheduling, we can develop more efficient, fair, and adaptive resource allocation strategies for next-generation wireless networks.


